{"name":"Vampiric Shadow","hex":"#bfb6aa","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vampiric","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the traits of a vampire."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vampire or vampire-like creature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vampiric"},{"term":"shadow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dark image projected onto a surface where light (or other radiation) is blocked by the shade of an object.","Relative darkness, especially as caused by the interruption of light; gloom; obscurity."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unofficial, informal, unauthorized, but acting as though it were.","Having power or influence, but not widely known or recognized."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shadow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vampire","blocks":[{"text":"A vampire is a mythical creature with a human-like appearance that subsists by feeding on the vital essence of humans. In European folklore, vampires are undead humanoid creatures that visit loved ones and cause mischief or deaths in the neighbourhoods they inhabited while they were alive. They wear shrouds and are often described as bloated and of ruddy or dark countenance, contrasting with the modern gaunt, pale vampire which dates from the early 19th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Philip_Burne-Jones_-_The_Vampire.jpg/330px-Philip_Burne-Jones_-_The_Vampire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vampire","wikidata":"Q46721"},{"title":"Shadow","blocks":[{"text":"A shadow is a dark area on a surface where light from a light source is blocked by an object. In contrast, shade occupies the three-dimensional volume behind an object with light in front of it. The cross-section of a shadow is a two-dimensional silhouette, or a reverse projection of the object blocking the light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/The_shadow_of_a_musician_playing_transverse_flute.jpg/330px-The_shadow_of_a_musician_playing_transverse_flute.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow","wikidata":"Q160020"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Edvard_Munch_-_Vampire_%281895%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vampire (1895) by Edvard Munch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEdvard_Munch_-_Vampire_(1895)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/72/Ernst6-thumb.gif/250px-Ernst6-thumb.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/72/Ernst6-thumb.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/72/Ernst6-thumb.gif/250px-Ernst6-thumb.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of a vampire from Max Ernst's Une Semaine de Bonté (1934)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AErnst6-thumb.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/GarlicBasket.jpg/500px-GarlicBasket.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_